Brazilian law does not mention hitchhiking (''carona''), and unlike most European codes it does not shut pedestrians out of the fast roads. [https://www.planalto.gov.br/ccivil_03/leis/l9503compilado.htm Article 68 of the ''Código de Trânsito Brasileiro''] (Law 9.503/1997) guarantees pedestrians the use of the ''acostamento'' — the hard shoulder — of rural roads, and § 3º provides that where there is no usable shoulder they may walk along the edge of the carriageway facing the traffic, with priority over vehicles. What is forbidden is the carriageway itself: article 254(I) bars a pedestrian from remaining or walking on the ''pistas de rolamento'' except to cross where crossing is permitted, which is a ''leve'' infraction. So standing on the ''acostamento'' of a ''rodovia'' with your thumb out is lawful, and hitchhiking here is done on the open road as much as at junctions — the ''postos'' and ''pedágios'' being where drivers already stop. Signage overrides all of this, and access roads and urban ''vias de trânsito rápido'' can be posted against pedestrians, so read the signs.
